  "body": "## Summary\n\nUsers without permissions could still reach management pages and see data they should not, and the group permission editor was a flat list of 20 switches that gave no sense of what any of them actually did. This PR fixes the scope server-side and rebuilds the editor as a permission matrix.\n\nCloses #6608\nCloses #6507\nCloses #5231\nCloses #2126\nCloses #5526\nCloses #4281\n\n## The access rule\n\nThere is now exactly one rule, and the navigation, the pages and the management-home statistics all consume the same function (`getAppManagementAccess` / `getIntegrationManagementAccess` in `@homarr/definitions`):\n\n> **A management section is reachable when you can do at least one thing in it, and it only ever lists what you are allowed to see.**\n\n| | `/manage/apps` | `/manage/integrations` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Manage all | full list, searchable, editable | full list |\n| Create only | empty state explaining you may only add, with the create action | empty state explaining you may only add, with the create action |\n| Delegated full access on specific integrations | \u2014 | only those integrations, above a note that the view is scoped |\n| None of the above | not found, with a link back to the management home | not found, with a link back to the management home |\n\nPreviously the nav could show a link to a page that returned not-found, and the stat cards were gated on unrelated permissions. They cannot drift now \u2014 that was the point of extracting the rule.\n\n## Server-side scope (the actual boundary)\n\n- `app.getPaginated` / `app.all` / `app.search` require `app-modify-all` (they were `protectedProcedure`, i.e. any signed-in user could enumerate every app name and URL \u2014 #5231).\n- `integration.all` / `allThatSupportSearch` / `allOfGivenCategory` / `search` filter **server-side**: without a global integration permission you only receive integrations you have access to. Delegated per-integration grants still work. `search` now applies its limit *after* filtering, so a scoped user gets a full page of results rather than a truncated one.\n- `home.getStats` counts only what the user may see.\n\n## Create is a capability, not an access level\n\nThis is the substantive correction to the original design of this PR.\n\nThe first version of the matrix modelled every category as a single ordered level (None \u2192 View \u2192 Modify \u2192 Full) and dropped the five create-shaped keys \u2014 `board-create`, `app-create`, `integration-create`, `search-engine-create`, `media-upload`. To compensate, `group.savePermissions` force-bundled each create key with a parent. Two consequences:\n\n- **Silent privilege escalation on upgrade.** A group holding `board-create` was rewritten to `board-full-all` \u2014 full control over every board, including deletion \u2014 on the next save of *any* permission. Same for `integration-create` \u2192 `integration-full-all`.\n- **A lost capability.** \"Members may create their own boards but not see anyone else's\" \u2014 probably the most common self-hosted configuration \u2014 became inexpressible, and applying a preset silently revoked existing create grants.\n\nCreating is orthogonal to how much of everyone else's data you can see, so it is now modelled that way: each row has an ordered access level **and** a checkbox per orthogonal **capability**. When the chosen level already implies a capability the checkbox is ticked and locked with a tooltip saying which level includes it. `savePermissions` stores exactly what was selected.\n\nA ladder may only contain keys that genuinely imply each other. `app-modify-all` implies `app-create` but **not** `app-use-all` \u2014 only `app-full-all` grants both \u2014 so the app row is `No access | Modify | Full` with *Use all apps* and *Create apps* as capabilities. Stacking use beneath modify would have made picking Modify silently grant the use of every app, which is the same fabrication in the other direction. A test asserts every ladder is a real chain, so this class of mismatch fails CI rather than shipping.\n\nAll 20 permission keys are representable and round-trip losslessly; there is a test asserting the matrix covers `groupPermissionKeys` exactly, so adding a permission without placing it in the matrix now fails CI.\n\n## Editor\n\n- Per-category access level, with short labels (View / Use / Interact / Modify / Full) and the selected level's own description underneath, so the meaning is on screen rather than in the docs.\n- A separate create checkbox per category.\n- Viewer / Editor / Admin presets with tooltips describing what each grants, and a Custom badge when the selection matches none.\n- \"Also grants: \u2026\" hints for implied permissions.\n- Choosing Administrator disables the other rows and explains why; setting it back to No access restores the previous selections.\n- Collapsible effective-permissions preview (the de-duplicated set including everything granted indirectly).\n- Fixes: saving left the unsaved-changes bar on screen (`setInitialValues` moves the values snapshot; only `resetDirty` clears the dirty flags), and a row showing only a create grant described itself as \"no access\".\n\n## Verification\n\nExercised against a local instance with a seeded group holding the legacy `board-create` + `media-upload` combination:\n\n- the two legacy keys render as checked, enabled create boxes and can be revoked \u2014 on `release/v2` they are invisible;\n- raising Boards to Full locks the create box, lowering it back to No access re-enables it still checked, so an explicit grant survives;\n- saving `board-create`, `app-create`, `integration-create`, `media-upload` persists exactly those four rows \u2014 the previous code also wrote `board-full-all`, `app-modify-all` and `integration-full-all`;\n- a user with only `app-create`/`integration-create` sees both nav entries and lands on the create-only empty state instead of a 404;\n- a user with no permissions sees no Apps/Integrations nav entries, no stat cards, and gets the not-found page with a back link on both routes;\n- the matrix stacks without overflow at 390px.\n\n## Tests\n\n`permissions.spec.ts` covers matrix coverage of every key, create implication per category, standalone create keys, normalisation of redundant create keys, round-trips, and the two access rules. `group.spec.ts` covers no-escalation for each create key, de-duplication, clearing and idempotency. Plus the existing FORBIDDEN cases for the gated app procedures and the integration filtering tests.\n\n## Docs\n\n`management/users`, `management/apps` and `management/integrations` describe the levels, which of them include create, and the access rule per page.\n\n## Review feedback addressed\n\n- **CodeRabbit** \u2014 `createPermissionParents` escalating create upward (removed), integrations nav hidden from create-only users (fixed by the shared access rule), `integration.search` limiting before filtering (fixed), the anonymous-session query in `getStats` (already guarded), and the API permission documentation (added).\n- **Codex** \u2014 the app ladder not matching the permission graph (fixed as described above), and the missing API migration guidance in `apps/docs/docs/management/api/index.mdx` (added: why app list endpoints can now return `FORBIDDEN`, and why integration lists may return fewer records).\n\n## Follow-ups (not in this PR)\n\n- `app.selectable` and `app.byIds` still return app names and URLs to any signed-in user. They back the board widget and bookmark pickers, so gating them needs its own change with its own testing.\n\n",
